00:01:48.290 --> 00:01:50.920
SHAHED LATIF: So let's talk
about key risks and threats

00:01:50.920 --> 00:01:52.960
to the cloud.

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:55.030
We thought we'd start
with a bit of a privacy

00:01:55.030 --> 00:01:56.890
and regulatory feel to it.

00:01:56.890 --> 00:02:00.250
And what we've done at PwC
is we've analyzed the market.

00:02:00.250 --> 00:02:04.010
We've looked at almost every
single country in the world.

00:02:04.010 --> 00:02:06.010
And we've identified
the number of drivers,

00:02:06.010 --> 00:02:09.084
environmental drivers,
international law, US law

00:02:09.084 --> 00:02:12.507
and regulations, enforcement and
case law, and self-regulation

00:02:12.507 --> 00:02:13.090
and standards.

00:02:13.090 --> 00:02:14.965
And you can see some of
the examples on here.

00:02:14.965 --> 00:02:16.413
And there's many, many more.

00:02:16.413 --> 00:02:18.580
What we find with our
clients, when we talk to them,

00:02:18.580 --> 00:02:20.920
they really don't
have enough insights

00:02:20.920 --> 00:02:23.920
because of the global nature of
the complexity of regulation.

00:02:23.920 --> 00:02:26.470
And sometimes it's
important to understand it.

00:02:26.470 --> 00:02:28.415
So we have developed
a point of view

00:02:28.415 --> 00:02:30.790
in almost all these countries
and have it in a repository

00:02:30.790 --> 00:02:31.570
at our fingertips.

00:02:31.570 --> 00:02:34.482
We don't need to spend hours
and weeks researching it.

00:02:34.482 --> 00:02:35.690
We have it at our fingertips.

00:02:35.690 --> 00:02:37.720
And we think that's
an important attribute

00:02:37.720 --> 00:02:40.400
to begin the cloud journey
to really understand that,

00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:42.400
especially when you talk
about data localization

00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:46.290
and where data might
physically reside.

00:02:46.290 --> 00:02:48.710
So what's the value,
though, of understanding

00:02:48.710 --> 00:02:51.350
all that regulation?

00:02:51.350 --> 00:02:53.780
What's the impact if
you have non-compliance?

00:02:53.780 --> 00:02:58.560
We think there's four broad
areas of non-compliance risks.

00:02:58.560 --> 00:03:01.040
The first is regulatory fines
and penalties, which I'm

00:03:01.040 --> 00:03:02.600
sure most of you are aware of.

00:03:02.600 --> 00:03:04.760
But you might be
subject to an FTC order.

00:03:04.760 --> 00:03:07.535
There's been a number of
consumer breaches that

00:03:07.535 --> 00:03:10.350
have occurred that have
resulted in decades long

00:03:10.350 --> 00:03:12.410
or multiple decades
of orders where

00:03:12.410 --> 00:03:17.690
you have to do audits for the
next 20 years in some cases.

00:03:17.690 --> 00:03:19.830
Some business operations
might be limited.

00:03:19.830 --> 00:03:22.520
So it could have a big impact
from a regulatory perspective.

00:03:22.520 --> 00:03:25.230
Financial risk-- you could
have loss of revenue.

00:03:25.230 --> 00:03:27.440
You could have litigation
costs, which is often

00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:30.250
the case in some breaches,
civil and criminal,

00:03:30.250 --> 00:03:33.150
and penalties for
data breaches as well.

00:03:33.150 --> 00:03:34.683
There's also a
reputational risk.

00:03:34.683 --> 00:03:35.850
And that's a big discussion.

00:03:35.850 --> 00:03:38.360
There's so many breaches
today that have occurred.

00:03:38.360 --> 00:03:39.860
Some people have
been numb to it.

00:03:39.860 --> 00:03:42.350
Share prices don't
really get impacted much.

00:03:42.350 --> 00:03:44.350
But does your
reputation get impacted?

00:03:44.350 --> 00:03:47.180
In some cases, it could,
especially if you're heavily

00:03:47.180 --> 00:03:49.380
relying on an online
presence or having

00:03:49.380 --> 00:03:53.277
to depend on technology where
security is really paramount.

00:03:53.277 --> 00:03:55.610
You might even lose lots of
confidence in your employees

00:03:55.610 --> 00:03:56.960
and start to get turnover.

00:03:56.960 --> 00:03:59.090
And then, finally,
operational risk--

00:03:59.090 --> 00:04:03.860
restricting operations,
transferring

00:04:03.860 --> 00:04:07.160
data in an appropriate way
or having an instant response

00:04:07.160 --> 00:04:10.940
program, having sort of
remediation or obligations

00:04:10.940 --> 00:04:13.160
to the instant response
program that didn't meet it.

00:04:13.160 --> 00:04:16.279
So these are some of
the non-compliance regs

00:04:16.279 --> 00:04:19.151
that you have to be careful of.

00:04:19.151 --> 00:04:22.037
I want to turn quickly to
some key considerations.

00:04:22.037 --> 00:04:24.620
We tried to summarize this in a
page, which is very difficult.

00:04:24.620 --> 00:04:28.610
But we think these are the main
things that we keep seeing over

00:04:28.610 --> 00:04:30.950
and over again at our clients.

00:04:30.950 --> 00:04:33.230
Governance and keeping pace--

00:04:33.230 --> 00:04:34.948
often, cybersecurity
is not always

00:04:34.948 --> 00:04:35.990
at the seat at the table.

00:04:35.990 --> 00:04:38.600
When it comes to
architectural discussions,

00:04:38.600 --> 00:04:41.300
IT may be at the front, or
business may be at the front.

00:04:41.300 --> 00:04:44.870
Rarely do I see sometimes cyber
is at a seat at the table.

00:04:44.870 --> 00:04:47.900
And with Google Cloud
adoption, you have to be.

00:04:47.900 --> 00:04:51.530
Collaborating on that solution
together to enhance enablement

00:04:51.530 --> 00:04:54.252
of agility through a
DevOp environment--

00:04:54.252 --> 00:04:56.460
and we'll talk later about
our thoughts around secure

00:04:56.460 --> 00:04:57.358
development--

00:04:57.358 --> 00:04:59.150
is really important to
bring that mentality

00:04:59.150 --> 00:05:02.330
that cyber is an equal
partner in the discussion.

00:05:02.330 --> 00:05:05.410
And modernizing the
employee skill sets--

00:05:05.410 --> 00:05:07.160
time and time again,
we go to our clients,

00:05:07.160 --> 00:05:09.290
and we find there's not
enough resources that

00:05:09.290 --> 00:05:10.670
know their subject.

00:05:10.670 --> 00:05:13.940
And we get called upon to
either augment the team

00:05:13.940 --> 00:05:17.120
or bring our specialized
skill set to the table.

00:05:17.120 --> 00:05:19.455
Identity access
management-- you probably

00:05:19.455 --> 00:05:21.830
have attended some of the
sessions over the last two days

00:05:21.830 --> 00:05:23.090
around this topic.

00:05:23.090 --> 00:05:25.220
But when you're in a
multi-cloud environment,

00:05:25.220 --> 00:05:27.110
it's quite complex to deal with.

00:05:27.110 --> 00:05:30.230
And throw on top of that
legacy applications as well.

00:05:30.230 --> 00:05:34.160
So centralizing the identity,
both the user and device,

00:05:34.160 --> 00:05:37.880
using MFA and PAM, are three
really important topics

00:05:37.880 --> 00:05:39.320
to address.

00:05:39.320 --> 00:05:41.420
Data protection and compliance--

00:05:41.420 --> 00:05:44.335
I mentioned earlier all the
regulations that's out there.

00:05:44.335 --> 00:05:46.460
Especially here, since
we're all here in California

00:05:46.460 --> 00:05:49.100
today, the CCP Act is
having a big impact

00:05:49.100 --> 00:05:51.320
on a lot of my clients,
especially those

00:05:51.320 --> 00:05:52.940
with a lot of consumer data.

00:05:52.940 --> 00:05:54.523
They're having to
understand now where

00:05:54.523 --> 00:05:57.370
is data, who has access to
it, and how do they use it.

00:05:57.370 --> 00:05:59.540
And if I have to delete
it, how can I delete it?

00:05:59.540 --> 00:06:03.200
So it's really important to have
an effective program around it.

00:06:03.200 --> 00:06:06.620
Legacy services-- what's
the path for data migration

00:06:06.620 --> 00:06:09.960
from legacy apps to a workload
that's going into the cloud?

00:06:09.960 --> 00:06:11.570
How do you deal with
data in transit?

00:06:11.570 --> 00:06:13.940
How do you encrypt
it the right way?

00:06:13.940 --> 00:06:15.750
And then preservation response--

00:06:15.750 --> 00:06:17.750
do you understand the
roles and responsibilities

00:06:17.750 --> 00:06:19.050
of the cloud provider?

00:06:19.050 --> 00:06:22.070
You might know that upfront
during early discussions.

00:06:22.070 --> 00:06:26.390
But as you get into month three,
month six, the second year,

00:06:26.390 --> 00:06:29.060
are you really clear on what
happens if there's a breach?

00:06:29.060 --> 00:06:30.365
Who's responsible for what?

00:06:30.365 --> 00:06:33.350
What are the SLAs around that
is really important to know.

00:06:35.930 --> 00:06:38.080
I want to quickly
talk now, jump into,

00:06:38.080 --> 00:06:40.510
something we've created
a PwC that's based

00:06:40.510 --> 00:06:42.880
on three basic principles.

00:06:42.880 --> 00:06:44.072
We looked hard.

00:06:44.072 --> 00:06:46.280
And we have a concept called
strategy three execution

00:06:46.280 --> 00:06:48.292
when we deliver our
solutions to a client.

00:06:48.292 --> 00:06:49.750
So when it came to
Google Cloud, we

00:06:49.750 --> 00:06:52.165
said we wanted to approach
it in three dimensions--

00:06:52.165 --> 00:06:55.810
a technical architecture,
which is probably a lot of what

00:06:55.810 --> 00:06:57.950
you saw in this conference.

00:06:57.950 --> 00:07:01.100
But we also feel it strongly
on a process architecture

00:07:01.100 --> 00:07:02.590
and a controls architecture.

00:07:02.590 --> 00:07:05.800
All three have to come
together and have to be linked.

00:07:05.800 --> 00:07:08.365
And that's what our view
is around, bringing it

00:07:08.365 --> 00:07:09.800
all together into one place.

00:07:09.800 --> 00:07:12.400
So I'm going to share with
you the next few slides how

00:07:12.400 --> 00:07:15.210
we've approached this topic.

00:07:15.210 --> 00:07:17.880
The first one is controls,
which is relatively easy.

00:07:17.880 --> 00:07:19.430
We've adopted NIST 8-53.

00:07:19.430 --> 00:07:21.430
We think that's the most
comprehensive framework

00:07:21.430 --> 00:07:23.450
out there, especially in the US.

00:07:23.450 --> 00:07:26.660
It's the most stringent
baseline to go after.

00:07:26.660 --> 00:07:29.210
We've coupled it,
obviously, with CIS version

00:07:29.210 --> 00:07:31.790
7, because there's a lot of the
technical hardening standards

00:07:31.790 --> 00:07:32.930
that you would have.

00:07:32.930 --> 00:07:36.440
We mapped them both together,
created specific controls

00:07:36.440 --> 00:07:37.240
for GCP.

00:07:37.240 --> 00:07:38.990
And we've identified
which ones are really

00:07:38.990 --> 00:07:40.820
critical and non-critical.

00:07:40.820 --> 00:07:43.700
And we also related which
ones are really technically

00:07:43.700 --> 00:07:45.800
automated, possible
through automation,

00:07:45.800 --> 00:07:48.520
which ones are really process
or workflow orientated,

00:07:48.520 --> 00:07:50.960
and which one would rely
on people to follow.

00:07:50.960 --> 00:07:52.970
We also took it
one step further.

00:07:52.970 --> 00:07:56.240
And we worked out which ones
are really native to within GCP

00:07:56.240 --> 00:07:57.610
and which ones are not.

00:07:57.610 --> 00:08:00.290
Often, our clients want to
maximize as much as possible

00:08:00.290 --> 00:08:01.640
from Google Cloud.

00:08:01.640 --> 00:08:04.615
And what we've done is try
to identify that early up.

00:08:04.615 --> 00:08:05.990
So having this
framework, we have

00:08:05.990 --> 00:08:09.150
a baseline of which to
measure our adoption against.

00:08:09.150 --> 00:08:11.120
So we think this is
one important attribute

00:08:11.120 --> 00:08:12.380
to have it defined.

00:08:12.380 --> 00:08:14.123
And you can see the
categories below.

00:08:14.123 --> 00:08:15.290
I won't go through them all.

00:08:15.290 --> 00:08:17.123
You can see them in
present in front of you.

00:08:17.123 --> 00:08:20.338
But this illustrates the
dimensions of controls

00:08:20.338 --> 00:08:21.380
that you need to address.

00:08:24.250 --> 00:08:26.158
I want to spend a few
minutes on this slide,

00:08:26.158 --> 00:08:27.950
because this is our
technical architecture.

00:08:27.950 --> 00:08:31.430
Underneath this, we have
content that talks about what

00:08:31.430 --> 00:08:34.807
does gut like from an
architectural perspective.

00:08:34.807 --> 00:08:36.890
We don't want to change
too much of what's already

00:08:36.890 --> 00:08:38.030
in the on-premise world.

00:08:38.030 --> 00:08:39.530
But what we've done
is translate it.

00:08:39.530 --> 00:08:42.470
What does it mean to
being in the cloud?

00:08:42.470 --> 00:08:44.660
So let me walk you through
how we were address

00:08:44.660 --> 00:08:46.340
to this technical architecture.

00:08:46.340 --> 00:08:49.310
First, we use the NIST
CSF domains through

00:08:49.310 --> 00:08:52.160
identify, protect, detach,
respond, and recover.

00:08:52.160 --> 00:08:54.410
We feel that most of our
clients, especially the board

00:08:54.410 --> 00:08:56.730
level, understand
that terminology.

00:08:56.730 --> 00:08:59.490
So we use that lens to
translate an executive level.

00:08:59.490 --> 00:09:01.490
Here, there are different
campaigns and programs

00:09:01.490 --> 00:09:04.097
that we're trying to
do using those domains.

00:09:04.097 --> 00:09:05.180
How do we measure success?

00:09:05.180 --> 00:09:07.910
We measure success
through that lens.

00:09:07.910 --> 00:09:10.490
We also want to take into
account the technology

00:09:10.490 --> 00:09:12.450
stack of the cloud environment.

00:09:12.450 --> 00:09:15.140
So there's compute, network,
storage, log-in monitoring,

00:09:15.140 --> 00:09:15.950
IAM.

00:09:15.950 --> 00:09:18.080
We look at it from that
lens in making sure we

00:09:18.080 --> 00:09:19.910
address those components.

00:09:19.910 --> 00:09:22.785
At the top, we also want to be
aware of the security risks.

00:09:22.785 --> 00:09:25.160
So these are inputs to each
of our technical requirements

00:09:25.160 --> 00:09:26.420
that we defined--

00:09:26.420 --> 00:09:31.130
visibility, business awareness,
compliance, data security,

00:09:31.130 --> 00:09:34.670
threat protection, cloud
IAM, instant response,

00:09:34.670 --> 00:09:37.790
vendor landscape, cloud
DevOps, vendor manager,

00:09:37.790 --> 00:09:40.910
cloud API security and
infrastructure security.

00:09:40.910 --> 00:09:43.070
We think it's important
that our model fully

00:09:43.070 --> 00:09:45.740
addresses those risks,
and they're embedded

00:09:45.740 --> 00:09:47.698
in our technical architecture.

00:09:47.698 --> 00:09:49.490
So I'll walk you through
them, and then I'm

00:09:49.490 --> 00:09:52.530
going to ask Gokul to share
some client experiences.

00:09:52.530 --> 00:09:54.400
So for example, IT
asset management--

00:09:54.400 --> 00:09:57.300
at a high level, everyone knows
that inventory is important.

00:09:57.300 --> 00:09:58.850
How do you do that
in the cloud when

00:09:58.850 --> 00:10:00.410
assets don't last
more than maybe

00:10:00.410 --> 00:10:02.180
a few hours or a few weeks?

00:10:02.180 --> 00:10:03.560
How do I get around that?

00:10:03.560 --> 00:10:05.390
And if I have to go
back and investigate,

00:10:05.390 --> 00:10:07.430
how do I deal with that?

00:10:07.430 --> 00:10:10.190
Threat and vulnerability
management--

00:10:10.190 --> 00:10:12.740
clients are struggling
sometimes in space.

00:10:12.740 --> 00:10:15.440
How do you do a
penetration test in GCP?

00:10:15.440 --> 00:10:17.780
And how do you do this
on a regular basis?

00:10:17.780 --> 00:10:20.780
What tools are available
for you to allow for that?

00:10:20.780 --> 00:10:23.120
Data protection--
how do we make sure

00:10:23.120 --> 00:10:26.540
that the data that's
there in the cloud

00:10:26.540 --> 00:10:29.120
is protected according
to your policies?

00:10:29.120 --> 00:10:33.020
Logging and monitoring, it's
really important to define it.

00:10:33.020 --> 00:10:35.637
Most of my clients have a
multi-cloud environment.

00:10:35.637 --> 00:10:37.220
What's the single
source of truth when

00:10:37.220 --> 00:10:38.345
it comes to the repository?

00:10:38.345 --> 00:10:39.730
How do we create
a data lake that

00:10:39.730 --> 00:10:41.560
will give us the
visibility that we need

00:10:41.560 --> 00:10:43.880
and in a real-time fashion?

00:10:43.880 --> 00:10:46.400
Instant response--
so if you ever

00:10:46.400 --> 00:10:48.290
do have a breach
of whatever nature,

00:10:48.290 --> 00:10:50.210
how do you respond quickly?

00:10:50.210 --> 00:10:52.620
Business continuity
and disaster recovery--

00:10:52.620 --> 00:10:55.790
how do we make sure that
you can recover quickly?

00:10:55.790 --> 00:10:57.830
We, ourselves, are
actually in the middle

00:10:57.830 --> 00:11:00.950
of researching some new ways
of looking at resiliency.

00:11:00.950 --> 00:11:02.930
So we're looking at
actually leavening

00:11:02.930 --> 00:11:04.340
some of Google Tech, right?

00:11:04.340 --> 00:11:07.070
And how do we quickly
assess dependencies

00:11:07.070 --> 00:11:10.050
on different assets
that are in the cloud?

00:11:10.050 --> 00:11:13.560
Governance and compliance--
important point

00:11:13.560 --> 00:11:17.010
of our governance is who
owns cloud architecture.

00:11:17.010 --> 00:11:19.560
Who approves cloud architecture?

00:11:19.560 --> 00:11:21.630
When it comes to
service level approvals,

00:11:21.630 --> 00:11:23.320
where do you get that defined?

00:11:23.320 --> 00:11:26.850
And now, you get a blurring
between operations, security,

00:11:26.850 --> 00:11:27.723
and development.

00:11:27.723 --> 00:11:29.140
How do you bring
all that together

00:11:29.140 --> 00:11:32.130
in a defined way that
doesn't bring down

00:11:32.130 --> 00:11:34.450
your levels of
security controls?

00:11:34.450 --> 00:11:38.550
Security development
lifecycle-- you

00:11:38.550 --> 00:11:41.250
may be surprised to hear
that companies are struggling

00:11:41.250 --> 00:11:45.660
with adopting an SDLC that's
applicable to a cloud-like

00:11:45.660 --> 00:11:47.050
environment.

00:11:47.050 --> 00:11:48.990
Risk assurance and
management-- how

00:11:48.990 --> 00:11:52.220
do you assure your customers,
your third parties,

00:11:52.220 --> 00:11:54.790
that you got a safe environment?

00:11:54.790 --> 00:11:56.757
IAM, Identity and
Access Management--

00:11:56.757 --> 00:11:57.840
I've talked about already.

00:11:57.840 --> 00:12:02.310
But central theme-- how you
control access into the cloud.

00:12:02.310 --> 00:12:04.602
Awareness and training and,
finally, network security--

00:12:04.602 --> 00:12:06.518
so I just wanted to spend
a little bit of time

00:12:06.518 --> 00:12:07.710
walking through each one.

00:12:07.710 --> 00:12:09.540
But I'm going to
ask Gokul now just

00:12:09.540 --> 00:12:12.090
to share some examples of
some of our client experiences

00:12:12.090 --> 00:12:13.452
as we've used this framework.

00:12:13.452 --> 00:12:14.410
GOKUL RAGHURAMAN: Sure.

00:12:14.410 --> 00:12:15.600
Sure, absolutely.

00:12:15.600 --> 00:12:18.390
So I'm going to take a
couple of domains here.

00:12:18.390 --> 00:12:21.360
And then we'll talk about
specific customer examples

00:12:21.360 --> 00:12:23.160
where we've been asked
to come in and help,

00:12:23.160 --> 00:12:24.712
and that's resulted
in our developing

00:12:24.712 --> 00:12:27.170
this kind of a framework to
help out in future engagements.

00:12:27.170 --> 00:12:31.260
So one of the first things
that we typically look at

00:12:31.260 --> 00:12:34.450
is what does a customer have
in their cloud environment.

00:12:34.450 --> 00:12:37.140
We recently were brought
in for a client who

00:12:37.140 --> 00:12:40.200
wanted to do a very quick
assessment on their environment

00:12:40.200 --> 00:12:42.250
and look at their readiness
of their environment.

00:12:42.250 --> 00:12:44.190
The first thing that
we ask them is, OK,

00:12:44.190 --> 00:12:45.630
what are you running
on the cloud?

00:12:45.630 --> 00:12:46.505
What are your assets?

00:12:46.505 --> 00:12:47.910
What are your resources?

00:12:47.910 --> 00:12:51.540
A lot of our clients have
an inventory available

00:12:51.540 --> 00:12:52.660
which they can use.

00:12:52.660 --> 00:12:55.560
But a lot of the times,
that's not accurate, right?

00:12:55.560 --> 00:12:58.500
And in the cloud, with the
scalability and the elasticity

00:12:58.500 --> 00:13:01.950
to it, there are resources
going up and down all the time.

00:13:01.950 --> 00:13:04.920
So how are you going to have
a framework and a process

00:13:04.920 --> 00:13:07.860
in place to be able
to measure and track

00:13:07.860 --> 00:13:09.900
that over a consistent
period of time, right?

00:13:09.900 --> 00:13:13.620
So I think the key
on the Google side

00:13:13.620 --> 00:13:16.230
is GCP has already provided
some native capabilities,

00:13:16.230 --> 00:13:17.220
like labeling.

00:13:17.220 --> 00:13:19.980
They have the Security
Command Center

00:13:19.980 --> 00:13:22.260
that's now integrated
with their asset lifecycle

00:13:22.260 --> 00:13:26.550
that you can leverage to enable
tagging on your resources,

00:13:26.550 --> 00:13:28.650
identifying what resources.

00:13:28.650 --> 00:13:31.830
And you build up a very
simple schematic model, which

00:13:31.830 --> 00:13:33.812
says, here is my resource.

00:13:33.812 --> 00:13:34.770
Here is what-- its use.

00:13:34.770 --> 00:13:35.730
Who is the owner?

00:13:35.730 --> 00:13:36.880
What is the environment?

00:13:36.880 --> 00:13:37.710
What is the purpose of that?

00:13:37.710 --> 00:13:39.900
And you have some level of
data classification associated

00:13:39.900 --> 00:13:40.540
to that.

00:13:40.540 --> 00:13:42.660
So once you have
established that model, that

00:13:42.660 --> 00:13:44.760
then becomes your baseline.

00:13:44.760 --> 00:13:47.880
And you start slowly building
automation on top of that.

00:13:47.880 --> 00:13:49.560
So once you have
that automation,

00:13:49.560 --> 00:13:53.610
it's very easy to start
building policies on top of that

00:13:53.610 --> 00:13:55.330
and you start enforcing
those policies.

00:13:55.330 --> 00:13:57.870
So the first thing
that we typically

00:13:57.870 --> 00:14:01.680
ask the customer is, what
is your asset inventory?

00:14:01.680 --> 00:14:05.610
And I would recommend, you
know, looking at your policies

00:14:05.610 --> 00:14:08.190
and your resource
management lifecycle

00:14:08.190 --> 00:14:12.310
to set up those labeling
policies in place.

00:14:12.310 --> 00:14:14.130
Another example
that we commonly run

00:14:14.130 --> 00:14:18.920
into when we walk into a
customer is traditionally you

00:14:18.920 --> 00:14:21.170
have these engineering
teams, the security team.

00:14:21.170 --> 00:14:23.030
Then you have the
app development team

00:14:23.030 --> 00:14:24.710
doing separate rules, right?

00:14:24.710 --> 00:14:27.440
As you go into the cloud with
all your automation in place,

00:14:27.440 --> 00:14:29.730
those lines have started
blurring all the more now,

00:14:29.730 --> 00:14:30.230
right?

00:14:30.230 --> 00:14:33.800
So you need to a proper
methodology and a process

00:14:33.800 --> 00:14:36.500
in place to be able
to say, how am I going

00:14:36.500 --> 00:14:38.180
to automate my infrastructure?

00:14:38.180 --> 00:14:40.650
How am I going to probation
my infrastructure?

00:14:40.650 --> 00:14:44.040
How do I embed security as part
of my development lifecycle,

00:14:44.040 --> 00:14:44.540
right?

00:14:44.540 --> 00:14:46.640
So somebody is
running containers,

00:14:46.640 --> 00:14:49.550
or somebody else is having
traditional workloads.

00:14:49.550 --> 00:14:51.740
How do I ensure a
consistent process

00:14:51.740 --> 00:14:54.560
that is baked in as part of
my build and release pipeline?

00:14:54.560 --> 00:14:58.550
So that's another thing
that we commonly notice.

00:14:58.550 --> 00:15:02.540
And we recommend coming up with
a proper process in place that

00:15:02.540 --> 00:15:04.640
works not only on your
on-prem environment,

00:15:04.640 --> 00:15:06.722
as well as on your
cloud environment.

00:15:06.722 --> 00:15:08.930
And typically, once you have
established the process,

00:15:08.930 --> 00:15:11.810
that can be tailored very
quickly to any of the cloud

00:15:11.810 --> 00:15:12.810
providers that you have.

00:15:12.810 --> 00:15:17.190
So that's a couple of
examples that I had in mind.

00:15:17.190 --> 00:15:18.740
So at this point
of time, I would

00:15:18.740 --> 00:15:21.500
like to invite Rob
from Google to join us

00:15:21.500 --> 00:15:22.960
for our quick fireside chat.

