00:16:16.760 --> 00:16:18.190
So, cool.

00:16:18.190 --> 00:16:20.190
SHAHED LATIF: So I think
now, Rob, what we'll do

00:16:20.190 --> 00:16:21.640
is turn to a fireside chat.

00:16:21.640 --> 00:16:23.698
We tried to think
about questions

00:16:23.698 --> 00:16:25.740
that people in the audience
might be thinking of.

00:16:25.740 --> 00:16:28.032
So Rob, why don't you start,
and we'll see where we go.

00:16:28.032 --> 00:16:28.823
ROB SADOWSKI: Yeah.

00:16:28.823 --> 00:16:30.690
I mean, I think the
interesting thing

00:16:30.690 --> 00:16:33.300
is that you have gone
through these implementations

00:16:33.300 --> 00:16:35.280
with many different
types of clients.

00:16:35.280 --> 00:16:38.340
And so early on in
those engagements,

00:16:38.340 --> 00:16:39.850
just when they're
considering cloud

00:16:39.850 --> 00:16:41.330
or whether they're
considering GCP,

00:16:41.330 --> 00:16:43.440
I mean, you talked
about technology.

00:16:43.440 --> 00:16:45.000
You talked about controls.

00:16:45.000 --> 00:16:47.130
You talked about process.

00:16:47.130 --> 00:16:50.370
Where are the biggest
challenges early

00:16:50.370 --> 00:16:54.030
on in the engagement or early
on in the process for people

00:16:54.030 --> 00:16:56.010
who may be either
starting that journey

00:16:56.010 --> 00:17:00.100
or who are very early
on in considering this?

00:17:00.100 --> 00:17:02.340
SHAHED LATIF: It's actually
an interesting question,

00:17:02.340 --> 00:17:04.710
because often we get the
inside look before even

00:17:04.710 --> 00:17:05.755
Google gets to the door.

00:17:05.755 --> 00:17:09.599
So with my largest clients,
we get asked all the time,

00:17:09.599 --> 00:17:11.160
is this the right product?

00:17:11.160 --> 00:17:12.730
Is this the right solution?

00:17:12.730 --> 00:17:16.200
So before even a cloud provider
gets to the table, we're asked,

00:17:16.200 --> 00:17:18.060
does it meet the
security requirements?

00:17:18.060 --> 00:17:20.609
And often, the ROI
is quickly addressed.

00:17:20.609 --> 00:17:23.280
That's not an issue, because
that's why we're at the table.

00:17:23.280 --> 00:17:25.319
Is this the right
transformation process?

00:17:25.319 --> 00:17:26.490
Absolutely.

00:17:26.490 --> 00:17:28.260
But the fundamental
question that's

00:17:28.260 --> 00:17:31.950
always the biggest lagger, is
it meeting my security policies?

00:17:31.950 --> 00:17:34.320
And often, what we find
is the client's security

00:17:34.320 --> 00:17:37.290
policies are written in a
way that doesn't translate

00:17:37.290 --> 00:17:40.110
to a Google-like environment.

00:17:40.110 --> 00:17:42.630
And so we're having to translate
their requirements and then

00:17:42.630 --> 00:17:45.120
what they're trying to do to
what the platform is going

00:17:45.120 --> 00:17:46.060
to bring them.

00:17:46.060 --> 00:17:48.840
And in some cases, yes,
they might quite be there.

00:17:48.840 --> 00:17:51.733
But 9 times out of 10, there's
a translation required.

00:17:51.733 --> 00:17:54.150
And so what we have been asked
to do, more often than not,

00:17:54.150 --> 00:17:56.670
is go in and try and
understand that complexity

00:17:56.670 --> 00:17:57.600
and bring simplicity.

00:17:57.600 --> 00:17:59.670
Because that's the other
side to the equation,

00:17:59.670 --> 00:18:01.980
bring the simplicity
and understand

00:18:01.980 --> 00:18:03.605
what the risks and
the vulnerabilities,

00:18:03.605 --> 00:18:05.438
but, more importantly,
the strengths that it

00:18:05.438 --> 00:18:06.130
brings as well.

00:18:06.130 --> 00:18:06.963
ROB SADOWSKI: Right.

00:18:06.963 --> 00:18:11.410
I mean, you talked about
basing your work on frameworks,

00:18:11.410 --> 00:18:13.590
like the NIST framework,
whether it does CSF or some

00:18:13.590 --> 00:18:14.590
of the control matrices.

00:18:14.590 --> 00:18:18.150
I mean, do you see people
basing their own policies

00:18:18.150 --> 00:18:23.670
around domains like that, or
do they tend to be looser?

00:18:23.670 --> 00:18:25.290
SHAHED LATIF: So it has varied.

00:18:25.290 --> 00:18:29.790
So I can give you a story
of a client, Fortune 15.

00:18:29.790 --> 00:18:33.570
So keep it within top 15,
I won't tell you which one.

00:18:33.570 --> 00:18:35.490
I showed up three years
ago at this client.

00:18:35.490 --> 00:18:40.940
They created their
policies on input

00:18:40.940 --> 00:18:43.550
from legal, input
from operations,

00:18:43.550 --> 00:18:47.570
but proprietary built, was
built off their own thinking.

00:18:47.570 --> 00:18:50.450
They asked me to come in and
evaluate not that but more

00:18:50.450 --> 00:18:51.723
their posture overall.

00:18:51.723 --> 00:18:54.140
And we concluded one of the
most important thing was to do

00:18:54.140 --> 00:18:56.223
was to actually base it
on a recognized framework.

00:18:56.223 --> 00:18:57.957
There we used NIST CSF.

00:18:57.957 --> 00:18:59.540
And more often than
not, we're finding

00:18:59.540 --> 00:19:01.910
NIST being the most common
framework to be used.

00:19:01.910 --> 00:19:04.790
ISO's still relevant,
especially from a global side.

00:19:04.790 --> 00:19:06.518
But the NIST is the
most relevant one.

00:19:06.518 --> 00:19:07.310
ROB SADOWSKI: Yeah.

00:19:07.310 --> 00:19:11.510
And I think that's one of the
reasons why, at Google Cloud,

00:19:11.510 --> 00:19:15.410
we try to do or we commit
to getting certifications

00:19:15.410 --> 00:19:18.090
among the most popular
international frameworks.

00:19:18.090 --> 00:19:21.230
So if someone is basing
their set of controls

00:19:21.230 --> 00:19:24.350
and how they meet policy
obligations based on something

00:19:24.350 --> 00:19:28.550
like ISO 27001 and then how
that gets translated through,

00:19:28.550 --> 00:19:30.290
there is often a direct
mapping that they

00:19:30.290 --> 00:19:34.367
can use to help them
understand within the confines

00:19:34.367 --> 00:19:36.950
of a shared responsibility model
what we're going to be doing,

00:19:36.950 --> 00:19:38.242
what they're going to be doing.

00:19:38.242 --> 00:19:40.780
And then we can also map
out to other controls there.

00:19:40.780 --> 00:19:43.280
SHAHED LATIF: Two other things
that I want to mention there.

00:19:43.280 --> 00:19:45.738
The other challenges with our
clients in the early adoption

00:19:45.738 --> 00:19:48.680
is, do I have the right
technical competency to it?

00:19:48.680 --> 00:19:51.360
Again, a big percentage
of our clients

00:19:51.360 --> 00:19:52.910
are building those
resources out.

00:19:52.910 --> 00:19:55.588
But they want to retool
their current staff to do it,

00:19:55.588 --> 00:19:57.380
which means they need
a leader and a change

00:19:57.380 --> 00:20:00.230
agent within the ranks to
be able to pull it through.

00:20:00.230 --> 00:20:02.840
And more often not, sometimes
they rely on a third party

00:20:02.840 --> 00:20:05.060
to fill in that gap sometimes--

00:20:05.060 --> 00:20:06.590
not always, but sometimes.

00:20:06.590 --> 00:20:08.210
But eventually,
they have to find

00:20:08.210 --> 00:20:11.240
that director or that
leader, whoever it is,

00:20:11.240 --> 00:20:13.530
to really drive
it to completion.

00:20:13.530 --> 00:20:16.250
ROB SADOWSKI: Do you find
that as organizations

00:20:16.250 --> 00:20:20.300
look to do some of this that
they are looking to actually

00:20:20.300 --> 00:20:21.710
bring existing controls?

00:20:21.710 --> 00:20:23.772
Like, they have a
control objective.

00:20:23.772 --> 00:20:25.730
But are they trying to
bring existing controls?

00:20:25.730 --> 00:20:29.580
Or are they relooking at
what is offered in the cloud?

00:20:29.580 --> 00:20:31.970
And I think we're going
to talk about some of this

00:20:31.970 --> 00:20:34.550
to say, OK, we can
replace this existing

00:20:34.550 --> 00:20:37.000
control that we have in place.

00:20:37.000 --> 00:20:38.810
And it may be based on
a particular product

00:20:38.810 --> 00:20:41.810
of how they meet that obligation
with something cloud native.

00:20:41.810 --> 00:20:43.310
SHAHED LATIF: I
think it's mentioned

00:20:43.310 --> 00:20:45.170
throughout this
conference, transformation.

00:20:45.170 --> 00:20:46.490
They want to automate.

00:20:46.490 --> 00:20:49.230
Automation is the
key to their success.

00:20:49.230 --> 00:20:51.230
They don't want a
compliance audit.

00:20:51.230 --> 00:20:52.435
They don't want controls.

00:20:52.435 --> 00:20:54.560
They want to automate,
which is a lot of what we're

00:20:54.560 --> 00:20:55.800
going to talk about later.

00:20:55.800 --> 00:20:58.820
They want to automate the
process that's involved

00:20:58.820 --> 00:21:00.450
in managing their environment.

00:21:00.450 --> 00:21:02.257
They don't want
to have hands on.

00:21:02.257 --> 00:21:03.590
They don't want people involved.

00:21:03.590 --> 00:21:05.632
They want to have machines
really do it for them.

00:21:05.632 --> 00:21:08.048
And some of the companies that
we're dealing with the size

00:21:08.048 --> 00:21:10.010
that they do, they
don't have a choice

00:21:10.010 --> 00:21:12.390
of the scale and the management
