00:21:43.980 --> 00:21:46.790
I mean, obviously, what
we announced with Anthos,

00:21:46.790 --> 00:21:49.250
where there is a lot
of security capability

00:21:49.250 --> 00:21:53.270
that is built in there-- whether
it's functionality like what's

00:21:53.270 --> 00:21:54.360
in Istio.

00:21:54.360 --> 00:21:59.360
So you can create and
monitor microservices

00:21:59.360 --> 00:22:02.360
that you can ensure
that you have encryption

00:22:02.360 --> 00:22:04.100
between those
different services,

00:22:04.100 --> 00:22:07.020
that you can automate
configurations.

00:22:07.020 --> 00:22:10.010
It was a capability like
configuration management

00:22:10.010 --> 00:22:14.090
where you are able to
define certain policies,

00:22:14.090 --> 00:22:15.620
instantiate those
policies, and make

00:22:15.620 --> 00:22:17.120
sure those are
pushed out at scale

00:22:17.120 --> 00:22:18.920
and make sure things
don't deviate.

00:22:18.920 --> 00:22:23.535
It's critically, I think,
important especially

00:22:23.535 --> 00:22:25.910
as organizations begin to
scale to make sure that they're

00:22:25.910 --> 00:22:28.310
adhering to policies,
that they're following

00:22:28.310 --> 00:22:29.520
good governance and so forth.

00:22:29.520 --> 00:22:30.780
So I think that was one.

00:22:30.780 --> 00:22:31.930
I think the second--

00:22:31.930 --> 00:22:34.700
Gokul, you mentioned Cloud
Security Command Center.

00:22:34.700 --> 00:22:37.340
And I think that
visibility into risk

00:22:37.340 --> 00:22:40.793
is something that many
organizations really,

00:22:40.793 --> 00:22:41.960
really have been focused on.

00:22:41.960 --> 00:22:43.793
As they move into the
cloud, how do I really

00:22:43.793 --> 00:22:47.270
understand the risk
profile of the assets that

00:22:47.270 --> 00:22:50.250
are there-- so the fact
that now in this service

00:22:50.250 --> 00:22:53.690
you get a full organization-wide
view of all your assets

00:22:53.690 --> 00:22:56.780
and then can begin to look
at the risk profile of each

00:22:56.780 --> 00:22:59.390
of those different
assets and kind of some

00:22:59.390 --> 00:23:01.160
of the other capabilities
where we brought

00:23:01.160 --> 00:23:05.030
on something like
security health analytics

00:23:05.030 --> 00:23:08.240
where we can say,
for those assets, are

00:23:08.240 --> 00:23:11.090
they configured in such
a way that is creating

00:23:11.090 --> 00:23:12.210
inordinate level of risk?

00:23:12.210 --> 00:23:15.503
Like, do we have storage buckets
with broad permissions that

00:23:15.503 --> 00:23:16.670
may be open to the internet?

00:23:16.670 --> 00:23:18.503
Do we have overly
permissive firewall rules?

00:23:18.503 --> 00:23:21.320
Do we have things that, again,
would put me in a situation

00:23:21.320 --> 00:23:25.580
where I would not be able to
meet the objective of a given

00:23:25.580 --> 00:23:26.570
policy?

00:23:26.570 --> 00:23:29.510
And I think back to
the automation point,

00:23:29.510 --> 00:23:31.850
something we talked about
in terms of event threat

00:23:31.850 --> 00:23:32.360
detection.

00:23:32.360 --> 00:23:34.790
How can we help automate
or at least provide

00:23:34.790 --> 00:23:37.160
some of the labor in
the detection process?

00:23:37.160 --> 00:23:39.245
So being able to take the logs--

00:23:39.245 --> 00:23:41.120
and I think we're going
to talk about logging

00:23:41.120 --> 00:23:42.495
and monitoring
here in a minute--

00:23:42.495 --> 00:23:46.700
but being able to take that
and actually use intelligence

00:23:46.700 --> 00:23:49.820
that we've gathered
to give you a sense of

00:23:49.820 --> 00:23:53.537
are there particular malicious
events or signs of compromise

00:23:53.537 --> 00:23:54.620
that you need to look for.

00:23:54.620 --> 00:24:00.020
And I think the final or
a couple others I think--

00:24:00.020 --> 00:24:00.950
around identity.

00:24:00.950 --> 00:24:03.680
I think that in terms of good
governance and implementing

00:24:03.680 --> 00:24:07.010
policies, identity is still one
of the biggest control points

00:24:07.010 --> 00:24:09.590
that we have in cloud
where we may not

00:24:09.590 --> 00:24:11.960
be able to implement
some of the controls

00:24:11.960 --> 00:24:14.965
that we have wanted them
to in terms of the network,

00:24:14.965 --> 00:24:17.090
because services are moving
up the stack that we're

00:24:17.090 --> 00:24:20.120
looking less and less at layer
3 and 4, and more and more

00:24:20.120 --> 00:24:21.260
at layer 7.

00:24:21.260 --> 00:24:26.618
How can we can really
get that level of control

00:24:26.618 --> 00:24:27.410
in the environment?

00:24:27.410 --> 00:24:29.900
And knowing who's
in the environment,

00:24:29.900 --> 00:24:32.090
being able to manage
that access appropriately

00:24:32.090 --> 00:24:35.300
throughout its lifecycle,
right at the beginning of that,

00:24:35.300 --> 00:24:36.240
it's authentication.

00:24:36.240 --> 00:24:37.910
So the fact that we
introduced the kind

00:24:37.910 --> 00:24:42.020
of being able to use the
Android phone as a security key,

00:24:42.020 --> 00:24:46.850
establishing that route trusted
identity at the beginning,

00:24:46.850 --> 00:24:49.393
I think is really,
really important.

00:24:49.393 --> 00:24:50.810
So those are a few
that stood out,

00:24:50.810 --> 00:24:54.320
especially within the context
of what we're doing here

00:24:54.320 --> 00:24:57.500
in terms of being
able to implement

00:24:57.500 --> 00:25:00.590
strong controls and strong
governance across the platform

00:25:00.590 --> 00:25:02.580
and really see how
effective those are.

00:25:02.580 --> 00:25:02.660
SHAHED LATIF: Yeah.

00:25:02.660 --> 00:25:04.430
One thing that stood out to me
in addition to what you've just

00:25:04.430 --> 00:25:06.032
said is the training aspect.

00:25:06.032 --> 00:25:08.240
I'm glad to see there's a
security certification now,

00:25:08.240 --> 00:25:09.440
eventually out.

00:25:09.440 --> 00:25:11.660
Because I think that's
absolutely needed out there.

00:25:11.660 --> 00:25:12.452
ROB SADOWSKI: Yeah.

00:25:12.452 --> 00:25:14.090
I mean, if folks
didn't see that,

00:25:14.090 --> 00:25:19.010
basically the week before we
introduced a specific security

00:25:19.010 --> 00:25:20.510
certification for GCP.

00:25:20.510 --> 00:25:23.270
And there's a set of coursework
that you can go and get

00:25:23.270 --> 00:25:26.380
that specialization.

00:25:26.380 --> 00:25:30.220
And a lot of that is driven
by demands that we had.

00:25:30.220 --> 00:25:33.200
We want to have more education
about the different security

00:25:33.200 --> 00:25:35.430
capabilities and how
they might be applied.

00:25:35.430 --> 00:25:37.880
And so that's a really
strong, I think,

00:25:37.880 --> 00:25:42.180
start as you begin to delve
more deeply into these issues.

00:25:42.180 --> 00:25:43.750
SHAHED LATIF: Great.

00:25:43.750 --> 00:25:45.640
ROB SADOWSKI: So
going back to you,

00:25:45.640 --> 00:25:50.890
Shahed and Gokul,
you know, you've

00:25:50.890 --> 00:25:53.230
gotten to the part of
advisory where people

00:25:53.230 --> 00:25:55.270
have decided to get into cloud.

00:25:55.270 --> 00:25:57.340
And they are saying, OK,
we have this workload.

00:25:57.340 --> 00:26:00.250
We have this type of data.

00:26:00.250 --> 00:26:03.480
What are they actually
looking to get in terms of pay

00:26:03.480 --> 00:26:04.335
off, right?

00:26:04.335 --> 00:26:04.960
Is it security?

00:26:04.960 --> 00:26:07.570
I mean, there's
this belief I think

00:26:07.570 --> 00:26:10.450
that has evolved over time
that moving to the cloud

00:26:10.450 --> 00:26:14.650
can actually potentially
increase your security posture

00:26:14.650 --> 00:26:18.850
or allow you to implement
stronger or better governance.

00:26:18.850 --> 00:26:21.242
Is that part of it, or is
it still something else?

00:26:21.242 --> 00:26:23.200
SHAHED LATIF: So there
are a number of drivers.

00:26:23.200 --> 00:26:24.760
And I think it varies
according to the industry

00:26:24.760 --> 00:26:26.740
you're in and also
the type of company

00:26:26.740 --> 00:26:28.990
you are in terms of your
style of management.

00:26:28.990 --> 00:26:33.210
But more often not, ROI is one
of the first drivers out there.

00:26:33.210 --> 00:26:35.870
And often, it's a data
center rationalization issue.

00:26:35.870 --> 00:26:38.380
So my clients have recognized
maintaining a data center

00:26:38.380 --> 00:26:40.830
today is really
expensive, not to mention

00:26:40.830 --> 00:26:42.370
they've got to make it secure.

00:26:42.370 --> 00:26:44.660
So that drives them
to a conclusion,

00:26:44.660 --> 00:26:46.520
I got to move more
things into the cloud.

00:26:46.520 --> 00:26:50.020
And then the question becomes,
what workloads can I put there?

00:26:50.020 --> 00:26:52.000
Over the years,
it's been very slow.

00:26:52.000 --> 00:26:55.930
But I worked with a fintech
client just recently.

00:26:55.930 --> 00:26:57.490
They moved their
entire production

00:26:57.490 --> 00:27:01.930
and all the entire platform
into Google Cloud and went live.

00:27:01.930 --> 00:27:04.300
And so those things
are changing.

00:27:04.300 --> 00:27:06.550
But there's still
a bigger majority

00:27:06.550 --> 00:27:09.200
of clients that have
not yet adopted cloud.

00:27:09.200 --> 00:27:11.590
So I think there's huge
growth potential still.

00:27:11.590 --> 00:27:15.640
So absolutely, ROI, but the
other thing is transformation.

00:27:15.640 --> 00:27:18.040
They're eager to
leverage some of the MI

00:27:18.040 --> 00:27:21.580
and the artificial intelligence
capability within compute

00:27:21.580 --> 00:27:24.040
to really harness
business transformation.

00:27:24.040 --> 00:27:27.060
That, even more than agility,
is driving some of the adoption.

00:27:27.060 --> 00:27:29.860
And those use cases are
driving the transformation

00:27:29.860 --> 00:27:32.170
which is driving our
business as well.

00:27:32.170 --> 00:27:34.870
ROB SADOWSKI: So let me push
on that point a little bit.

00:27:34.870 --> 00:27:38.860
I mean, do you see clients who
feel that moving to the cloud

00:27:38.860 --> 00:27:42.010
either enhances their
security posture

00:27:42.010 --> 00:27:45.172
or allows them to have stronger,
more effective governance?

00:27:45.172 --> 00:27:47.380
SHAHED LATIF: Personal view,
rather than a firm view,

00:27:47.380 --> 00:27:49.838
just because I don't want to
say too much on the firm half,

00:27:49.838 --> 00:27:52.580
but I personally believe
that from my experience--

00:27:52.580 --> 00:27:55.450
and I've been in security
consulting over 30 years--

00:27:55.450 --> 00:28:01.000
that going to a reputable
cloud solution such as Google

00:28:01.000 --> 00:28:03.370
is absolutely more
beneficial than trying

00:28:03.370 --> 00:28:04.780
to build it yourself.

00:28:04.780 --> 00:28:07.720
And when you think about the
complexities of the emerging

00:28:07.720 --> 00:28:10.330
tech that's about
to hit us, you need

00:28:10.330 --> 00:28:13.220
to be with a provider that
can give you that capability.

00:28:13.220 --> 00:28:14.900
ROB SADOWSKI: OK.

00:28:14.900 --> 00:28:17.770
SHAHED LATIF: So, Rob,
how does Google initially,

00:28:17.770 --> 00:28:21.040
at the beginning when you're
adopting Google Cloud,

00:28:21.040 --> 00:28:23.120
how do you help in
those initial stages

00:28:23.120 --> 00:28:24.550
in creating those
business cases?

00:28:24.550 --> 00:28:26.883
And how do you help to meet
their internal requirements,

00:28:26.883 --> 00:28:28.898
especially on the
first few months when

00:28:28.898 --> 00:28:30.190
they're getting off the ground?

00:28:30.190 --> 00:28:30.430
ROB SADOWSKI: Yeah.

00:28:30.430 --> 00:28:32.560
I mean, I think that's
a nice segue, right?

00:28:32.560 --> 00:28:35.950
Because one of the things
that we try to do is

00:28:35.950 --> 00:28:39.070
show that level of security
enhancement or security

00:28:39.070 --> 00:28:39.610
benefit.

00:28:39.610 --> 00:28:42.430
So you know, typically,
what would happen?

00:28:42.430 --> 00:28:43.990
Very early on, we
understand what

00:28:43.990 --> 00:28:45.490
are the different
types of services,

00:28:45.490 --> 00:28:48.302
or what is the workload,
or what is the application.

00:28:48.302 --> 00:28:50.260
And from there, we
understand a little bit more

00:28:50.260 --> 00:28:51.385
about the business process.

00:28:51.385 --> 00:28:53.860
We understand a little bit
more about the type of data.

00:28:53.860 --> 00:28:56.208
And then what we want to
do, back to kind of what you

00:28:56.208 --> 00:28:58.000
are saying and why I
was asking about that,

00:28:58.000 --> 00:29:01.150
is understand what are
the security policies that

00:29:01.150 --> 00:29:02.140
are around that data.

00:29:02.140 --> 00:29:04.330
And where it gets
challenging, in some cases,

00:29:04.330 --> 00:29:07.210
is if the policy isn't
particularly well-defined

00:29:07.210 --> 00:29:09.130
or if there aren't stated
control objectives.

00:29:09.130 --> 00:29:13.180
Because what we like to then do
is be able to give an overview

00:29:13.180 --> 00:29:15.010
or give a sense
of, OK, here's how

00:29:15.010 --> 00:29:18.630
you can use the features in
the platform to protect this.

00:29:18.630 --> 00:29:20.740
Here's how you can
meet your obligations

00:29:20.740 --> 00:29:23.890
from an internal policy
or also from potentially

00:29:23.890 --> 00:29:25.920
an external regulatory
perspective and the ways

00:29:25.920 --> 00:29:26.920
that we can help, right?

00:29:26.920 --> 00:29:30.100
And that's when we talk about
the relevant certifications

00:29:30.100 --> 00:29:32.980
or the relevant documentation
we have around some

00:29:32.980 --> 00:29:34.450
of those different things.

00:29:34.450 --> 00:29:37.270
So we really like to have the
security conversation upfront,

00:29:37.270 --> 00:29:40.630
because we know that it becomes
harder and harder to drive

00:29:40.630 --> 00:29:42.740
change if there are
questions about risk,

00:29:42.740 --> 00:29:47.080
if there are questions about
being able to meet obligations

00:29:47.080 --> 00:29:48.170
and guidance and so forth.

00:29:48.170 --> 00:29:50.140
So I think that that's
a big part of it.

00:29:50.140 --> 00:29:55.000
Another thing that we like to
do early on in the engagement

00:29:55.000 --> 00:29:58.120
is to even go far
as to do workshops.

00:29:58.120 --> 00:30:01.930
And that may be with partners
like yourselves or internally

00:30:01.930 --> 00:30:05.650
with our folks where, again,
we look at internal policies.

00:30:05.650 --> 00:30:07.120
We interview stakeholders.

00:30:07.120 --> 00:30:10.370
We understand
security objectives.

00:30:10.370 --> 00:30:12.370
We do some assessment
of existing controls.

00:30:12.370 --> 00:30:15.460
Because sometimes
the as is environment

00:30:15.460 --> 00:30:17.360
doesn't match the
desired future state.

00:30:17.360 --> 00:30:19.870
And so we can do a
little bit of solution

00:30:19.870 --> 00:30:22.240
and designing ahead
of time, and then

00:30:22.240 --> 00:30:24.730
kind of come up with
a roadmap as to some

00:30:24.730 --> 00:30:27.190
of the things that are
must implement and then

00:30:27.190 --> 00:30:28.880
phase for other pieces.

00:30:28.880 --> 00:30:31.220
So that's what we
try to do early on.

00:30:31.220 --> 00:30:33.550
But, clearly, security
has to be an integrated

00:30:33.550 --> 00:30:36.070
part of that design decision.

00:30:36.070 --> 00:30:38.350
I mean, just like the
philosophy of when

00:30:38.350 --> 00:30:42.340
we are doing CI/CD of
shifting security left,

00:30:42.340 --> 00:30:45.460
you're shifting security left
in the engagement itself.

00:30:45.460 --> 00:30:47.150
So we are planning
and not saying,

00:30:47.150 --> 00:30:48.880
oh, now we have this workload.

00:30:48.880 --> 00:30:52.540
Wait, you know, how are we going
to meet the obligations that we

00:30:52.540 --> 00:30:53.498
have?

00:30:53.498 --> 00:30:55.540
SHAHED LATIF: Who is the
most engaging party when

00:30:55.540 --> 00:30:58.120
you go to a client, which
function-- security,

00:30:58.120 --> 00:31:01.332
IT, business-- that you feel
is most closest the top?

00:31:01.332 --> 00:31:03.040
ROB SADOWSKI: It has
to be all the above.

00:31:03.040 --> 00:31:07.840
But I mean, I think that what
we see the most is the security

00:31:07.840 --> 00:31:12.970
function is seen as the
team or the center that

00:31:12.970 --> 00:31:15.050
is going to sign off on
a lot of what happens.

00:31:15.050 --> 00:31:16.978
So that's where a lot
of the gravity is.

00:31:16.978 --> 00:31:19.270
But, again, in doing interviews
and understanding that,

00:31:19.270 --> 00:31:21.130
we have to understand
from the business side

00:31:21.130 --> 00:31:23.560
and from other parts who
have influence over it.

00:31:23.560 --> 00:31:25.940
Because everyone
needs to be bought in.

00:31:25.940 --> 00:31:27.520
SHAHED LATIF: I don't know how
familiar you are with the three

00:31:27.520 --> 00:31:29.670
day discovery programs I
just recently found out

00:31:29.670 --> 00:31:32.380
about that you actually spend
three days with a client

00:31:32.380 --> 00:31:34.930
and actually do workshops and
educate them around security.

00:31:34.930 --> 00:31:36.820
I don't know if you want to
talk a little bit about that.

00:31:36.820 --> 00:31:36.940
ROB SADOWSKI: Yeah.

00:31:36.940 --> 00:31:39.220
I mean, again, it's a kind
of workshopping process

00:31:39.220 --> 00:31:41.560
where we can do some
education about the controls

00:31:41.560 --> 00:31:46.999
and contextualize it in terms
of the existing business.

00:31:46.999 --> 00:31:47.707
SHAHED LATIF: OK.

00:31:50.590 --> 00:31:51.590
ROB SADOWSKI: Let's see.

00:31:51.590 --> 00:31:55.330
So what else did I
want to get to here?

00:31:55.330 --> 00:31:58.770
So, again, getting more
to the specific controls

00:31:58.770 --> 00:32:02.200
and technologies, what
are the biggest challenges

00:32:02.200 --> 00:32:04.000
in terms of the technical side?

00:32:04.000 --> 00:32:07.270
What is specific security
controls or pieces

00:32:07.270 --> 00:32:10.090
of functionality are they
looking to implement and are

00:32:10.090 --> 00:32:12.160
they challenged to implement?

00:32:12.160 --> 00:32:14.092
SHAHED LATIF: So DLP was one.

00:32:14.092 --> 00:32:15.550
And that just
recently got embedded

00:32:15.550 --> 00:32:17.065
into the roadmap within there.

00:32:17.065 --> 00:32:18.940
But, again, one of my
clients was challenged.

00:32:18.940 --> 00:32:21.620
It wasn't available at
the time, but now it is.

00:32:21.620 --> 00:32:23.260
But DLP was one challenge.

00:32:23.260 --> 00:32:26.320
And that's really important when
you deal with piracy matters.

00:32:26.320 --> 00:32:31.650
SIM and UBA-- so with insider
threat and for detection,

00:32:31.650 --> 00:32:34.060
UBA is really important,
User Behavior Analytics.

00:32:34.060 --> 00:32:36.417
And how do you use an
existing SIM platform?

00:32:36.417 --> 00:32:38.500
Using stack driver or the
Security Command Center,

00:32:38.500 --> 00:32:40.417
how do I use that with
their existing platform

00:32:40.417 --> 00:32:41.830
that they might have?

00:32:41.830 --> 00:32:43.330
Container security--
and obviously,

00:32:43.330 --> 00:32:45.910
Anthos is a great example of
bringing a multi-cloud feature

00:32:45.910 --> 00:32:46.600
to it.

00:32:46.600 --> 00:32:49.630
But managing your operations
around the new technology that

00:32:49.630 --> 00:32:52.370
brings will be interesting.

00:32:52.370 --> 00:32:55.810
Endpoint detection
and response, that's

00:32:55.810 --> 00:32:57.960
a challenge for most
clients even on on-premise.

00:32:57.960 --> 00:32:59.710
How do you do it in a
virtual environment?

00:32:59.710 --> 00:33:01.900
And how do you get real-time
indications of what's

00:33:01.900 --> 00:33:03.460
going on in that space?

00:33:03.460 --> 00:33:06.767
Firewall rule processes--
changes made to firewalls

00:33:06.767 --> 00:33:08.350
when you're in a
developer environment

00:33:08.350 --> 00:33:09.370
are made very quickly.

00:33:09.370 --> 00:33:10.930
But it may not be done right.

00:33:10.930 --> 00:33:12.802
One of my other clients
that I worked with,

00:33:12.802 --> 00:33:14.260
they changed their
firewalls really

00:33:14.260 --> 00:33:16.660
quickly to the point
where it created problems.

00:33:16.660 --> 00:33:18.890
Because they're in a dev
environment making quickly.

00:33:18.890 --> 00:33:21.040
So how do you create
controls around changes?

00:33:21.040 --> 00:33:23.485
Which sounds obvious, but I'm
talking about a live example

00:33:23.485 --> 00:33:26.080
that that wasn't the case.

00:33:26.080 --> 00:33:27.575
And then configuration
management,

00:33:27.575 --> 00:33:28.450
that's the other one.

00:33:28.450 --> 00:33:30.700
With so many
configurations, the ability

00:33:30.700 --> 00:33:33.415
to make so many changes,
how do you manage that?

00:33:33.415 --> 00:33:34.630
Can that be scripted?

00:33:34.630 --> 00:33:35.848
Can we control that?

00:33:35.848 --> 00:33:36.640
ROB SADOWSKI: Yeah.

00:33:36.640 --> 00:33:42.740
I mean, I think that
clearly the rapidity,

00:33:42.740 --> 00:33:44.750
the ease of making changes--

00:33:44.750 --> 00:33:47.160
which is great, because it
allows you to be responsive.

00:33:47.160 --> 00:33:49.755
But it also can cause
configuration drift

00:33:49.755 --> 00:33:50.880
and other things like that.

00:33:50.880 --> 00:33:52.058
So we try to.

00:33:52.058 --> 00:33:53.600
And that's the
technology side thing,

00:33:53.600 --> 00:33:55.070
and it's also a
process side thing.

00:33:55.070 --> 00:33:57.930
But it's certainly not
something to be underestimated.

00:33:57.930 --> 00:33:59.480
And I think we see that as well.

00:33:59.480 --> 00:34:02.263
SHAHED LATIF: So maybe, what are
the native solutions within GCP

00:34:02.263 --> 00:34:04.430
that you think can address
some of the things I just

00:34:04.430 --> 00:34:04.980
brought up?

00:34:04.980 --> 00:34:05.772
ROB SADOWSKI: Yeah.

00:34:05.772 --> 00:34:07.700
I mean, clearly we
have a lot of controls

00:34:07.700 --> 00:34:10.075
that can address many of those
different types of things.

00:34:10.075 --> 00:34:13.949
You talk about behavioral
analysis and things like that.

00:34:13.949 --> 00:34:16.130
One of the things we
talked about this week

00:34:16.130 --> 00:34:20.431
was our policy intelligence
tool that we're bringing out.

00:34:20.431 --> 00:34:21.889
And so one of the
things that we're

00:34:21.889 --> 00:34:25.400
doing there is looking
at the set of IAMs roles

00:34:25.400 --> 00:34:27.230
that have been defined
and then looking

00:34:27.230 --> 00:34:30.080
at access that's happening
and being able to say,

00:34:30.080 --> 00:34:31.020
is there a mismatch.

00:34:31.020 --> 00:34:32.870
So even if a role is
defined correctly,

00:34:32.870 --> 00:34:35.510
we can say this group
of users have not

00:34:35.510 --> 00:34:36.889
accessed this resource.

00:34:36.889 --> 00:34:38.480
Should we remove
this permission--

00:34:38.480 --> 00:34:41.330
so getting to more
effective governance

00:34:41.330 --> 00:34:45.409
based on user behavior and
looking at that over time.

00:34:45.409 --> 00:34:48.114
I think, as you mentioned
container security,

00:34:48.114 --> 00:34:50.239
there are a number of
capabilities we talked about.

00:34:50.239 --> 00:34:54.139
We talked this week about what
we are doing round container

00:34:54.139 --> 00:34:55.639
registry vulnerability
scanning, so

00:34:55.639 --> 00:34:58.430
being able to scan
for vulnerabilities

00:34:58.430 --> 00:35:02.030
in the development process
and provide attestations

00:35:02.030 --> 00:35:05.560
of whether particular components
or other underlying things

00:35:05.560 --> 00:35:06.830
have vulnerabilities in them.

00:35:06.830 --> 00:35:10.700
And then very much related to
that is a tool that is now GA

00:35:10.700 --> 00:35:13.430
called binary authorization--
so, in that process,

00:35:13.430 --> 00:35:17.660
making sure that code is
signed and checked through

00:35:17.660 --> 00:35:20.360
based on a variety of
criteria before it actually

00:35:20.360 --> 00:35:22.310
goes to the deploy state.

00:35:22.310 --> 00:35:27.980
So this whole idea of giving
you tooling to control and get

00:35:27.980 --> 00:35:30.590
oversight over your
software supply chain

00:35:30.590 --> 00:35:32.360
is critically important
as organizations

00:35:32.360 --> 00:35:34.700
are going to transform
and modernize

00:35:34.700 --> 00:35:36.110
some of these dev processes.

00:35:36.110 --> 00:35:39.500
And those are two
capabilities that

00:35:39.500 --> 00:35:41.100
are kind of built in there.

00:35:41.100 --> 00:35:42.110
SHAHED LATIF: So let's
take a pause for a minute.

00:35:42.110 --> 00:35:43.700
Because Gokul's been
quiet on the end,

00:35:43.700 --> 00:35:45.110
and I want to bring him in.

00:35:45.110 --> 00:35:47.410
So I want a deep dive
a little bit more

00:35:47.410 --> 00:35:49.160
into identity access
management, because I

00:35:49.160 --> 00:35:50.900
think it's a core
capability that needs

00:35:50.900 --> 00:35:52.430
to be really thought through.

00:35:52.430 --> 00:35:54.890
Maybe, Gokul, I'm
going to share a slide

00:35:54.890 --> 00:35:56.630
that you can maybe illustrate.

00:35:56.630 --> 00:35:58.250
Tell us a little
bit more about what

00:35:58.250 --> 00:36:01.530
do you need to have to have
an effective IAM initiative.

00:36:01.530 --> 00:36:03.470
GOKUL RAGHURAMAN: Sure.

00:36:03.470 --> 00:36:07.563
So IAM is one of the core tenets
of when we go into a customer

00:36:07.563 --> 00:36:09.980
site and they want to build
out their cloud transformation

00:36:09.980 --> 00:36:10.550
program.

00:36:10.550 --> 00:36:12.890
IAM is one of the core
foundational capabilities

00:36:12.890 --> 00:36:14.030
that we start with.

00:36:14.030 --> 00:36:17.930
So if you don't get the IAM and
the resource hierarchy right,

00:36:17.930 --> 00:36:19.640
then it causes a
bigger challenge

00:36:19.640 --> 00:36:21.840
as you start scaling
up your business.

00:36:21.840 --> 00:36:26.060
So some of the basic
tenets, as we look at IAM,

00:36:26.060 --> 00:36:29.520
is how do you organize
your resources in Google?

00:36:29.520 --> 00:36:31.860
What are the different
capability services that

00:36:31.860 --> 00:36:33.720
are available out of the box?

00:36:33.720 --> 00:36:36.030
What considerations do you
need to be thinking about

00:36:36.030 --> 00:36:37.890
as you organize your projects?

00:36:37.890 --> 00:36:40.725
And your folder structure
and your resource structure

00:36:40.725 --> 00:36:42.600
in your Google environment
is the first thing

00:36:42.600 --> 00:36:43.540
that we look at.

00:36:43.540 --> 00:36:46.210
So what do I mean by that?

00:36:46.210 --> 00:36:48.880
Google has a resource
hierarchy which

00:36:48.880 --> 00:36:51.280
starts with
organization resource,

00:36:51.280 --> 00:36:52.327
and then there's folders.

00:36:52.327 --> 00:36:53.410
And then there's projects.

00:36:53.410 --> 00:36:56.080
And then there's
underlying resources

00:36:56.080 --> 00:36:59.410
to compute your network,
all those resources.

00:36:59.410 --> 00:37:03.250
So typically, when we
go in at the root level,

00:37:03.250 --> 00:37:06.560
organization is the node that
customers typically start with,

00:37:06.560 --> 00:37:07.060
right?

00:37:07.060 --> 00:37:11.110
And if you have a G Suite
account or an Cloud Identity

00:37:11.110 --> 00:37:14.920
account, that organization
resource is available to you.

00:37:14.920 --> 00:37:16.960
The advantage of
that resource is

00:37:16.960 --> 00:37:20.530
you can now start
tying your policies

00:37:20.530 --> 00:37:24.743
and your controls at the
organizational level.

00:37:24.743 --> 00:37:26.410
In some cases, it's
actually beneficial.

00:37:26.410 --> 00:37:29.180
You don't have to duplicate
them across multiple projects.

00:37:29.180 --> 00:37:31.550
So that's the root
level that you get.

00:37:31.550 --> 00:37:34.810
And typically, once you start
at the organization level,

00:37:34.810 --> 00:37:38.900
any projects that you create are
then automatically associated

00:37:38.900 --> 00:37:41.170
to that domain and then,
as part of that, associated

00:37:41.170 --> 00:37:42.680
to your organization.

00:37:42.680 --> 00:37:44.530
So if somebody leaves
your organization,

00:37:44.530 --> 00:37:46.822
you already have that
associated to your organization.

00:37:46.822 --> 00:37:48.280
It follows the
organization and not

00:37:48.280 --> 00:37:51.670
the individual who's actually
leaving the organization.

00:37:51.670 --> 00:37:54.240
So from an administrative
perspective,

00:37:54.240 --> 00:37:55.540
it's centralized management.

00:37:55.540 --> 00:37:59.590
It allows you to manage your
resources and your controls

00:37:59.590 --> 00:38:01.400
and the policies in
a centralized manner.

00:38:01.400 --> 00:38:04.120
So that's why having that
organizational resource

00:38:04.120 --> 00:38:06.250
helps you set the precedents.

00:38:06.250 --> 00:38:09.490
So then we have this concept
of folders resources.

00:38:09.490 --> 00:38:13.480
So a lot of times
our customers want

00:38:13.480 --> 00:38:17.770
to segregate their projects
by functional units,

00:38:17.770 --> 00:38:20.000
by departments, by environment.

00:38:20.000 --> 00:38:22.870
So that's where the concept
of folders is very helpful.

00:38:22.870 --> 00:38:25.570
So I might have a
marketing department.

00:38:25.570 --> 00:38:28.080
I might have another
legal department.

00:38:28.080 --> 00:38:30.280
I want specific policies
associated to that.

00:38:30.280 --> 00:38:33.100
I'm want projects which
have specific controls,

00:38:33.100 --> 00:38:36.110
IAM policies related only for
that particular department,

00:38:36.110 --> 00:38:36.610
right?

00:38:36.610 --> 00:38:38.910
So that's where I use
the concept of folders

00:38:38.910 --> 00:38:41.140
to groups similar
functional units

00:38:41.140 --> 00:38:45.830
or similar functions together
as part of the folder structure.

00:38:45.830 --> 00:38:48.550
Then you have the
actual project, which

00:38:48.550 --> 00:38:50.290
derives from the folder, right?

00:38:50.290 --> 00:38:51.760
And folder is an optional level.

00:38:51.760 --> 00:38:54.460
You don't have to have
that folder structure,

00:38:54.460 --> 00:38:56.080
but we typically
recommend that if you

00:38:56.080 --> 00:38:59.840
want a bunch or group together
similar functional units.

00:38:59.840 --> 00:39:02.960
At the project level, that's
where the real work actually

00:39:02.960 --> 00:39:03.460
happens.

00:39:03.460 --> 00:39:05.830
That's when you stand
up your resources.

00:39:05.830 --> 00:39:07.210
You spin up your applications.

00:39:07.210 --> 00:39:08.960
That's where you deploy
everything, right?

00:39:08.960 --> 00:39:12.180
So the project is the primary
control that you have.

00:39:12.180 --> 00:39:16.190
Now, once you have set up
this organizational structure

00:39:16.190 --> 00:39:18.030
in place, typically
our customers say,

00:39:18.030 --> 00:39:20.420
so what is the
recommended project

00:39:20.420 --> 00:39:22.100
structure that I need to have?

00:39:22.100 --> 00:39:24.890
Do I need to have
it by environment?

00:39:24.890 --> 00:39:27.070
Do I need to have
it by department?

00:39:27.070 --> 00:39:29.510
Again, typical answer
is it depends, right?

00:39:29.510 --> 00:39:33.470
So if you are an
organization, unless you have

00:39:33.470 --> 00:39:35.720
very specific requirements--

00:39:38.250 --> 00:39:40.460
so let's say I have
an organization.

00:39:40.460 --> 00:39:42.800
And there is
another organization

00:39:42.800 --> 00:39:46.460
which has completely different
set of policies and functions,

00:39:46.460 --> 00:39:48.200
and I want complete isolation.

00:39:48.200 --> 00:39:50.690
That's when you really need
two separate organizations.

00:39:50.690 --> 00:39:53.250
Otherwise, one organization
is the way to go.

00:39:53.250 --> 00:39:55.238
One domain is the way to go.

00:39:55.238 --> 00:39:56.780
And then from a
project level, that's

00:39:56.780 --> 00:39:59.270
where you start
segregating projects.

00:39:59.270 --> 00:40:03.830
So I could have projects created
for dev environment, for my QA,

00:40:03.830 --> 00:40:05.127
or my production environment.

00:40:05.127 --> 00:40:06.960
Or I could also slice
it in a different way,

00:40:06.960 --> 00:40:09.650
say I need my project
structure to be set up

00:40:09.650 --> 00:40:11.000
by my functional units.

00:40:11.000 --> 00:40:13.970
My marketing, you have a certain
set of project hierarchy.

00:40:13.970 --> 00:40:16.760
You have certain users,
roles, policies assigned

00:40:16.760 --> 00:40:18.080
by that functional unit.

00:40:18.080 --> 00:40:20.038
So that's the way that
we typically look at it.

00:40:20.038 --> 00:40:22.160
So once you get
that right, then you

00:40:22.160 --> 00:40:28.130
start thinking about the
concept of identity and roles.

00:40:28.130 --> 00:40:30.620
So really there is two
kinds of identity in GCP.

00:40:30.620 --> 00:40:32.510
One is the user identity.

00:40:32.510 --> 00:40:34.970
And then there is a
service account identity.

00:40:34.970 --> 00:40:37.750
So users are pretty
much all your users,

00:40:37.750 --> 00:40:39.473
the individual
users who are going

00:40:39.473 --> 00:40:40.640
to be using the environment.

00:40:40.640 --> 00:40:42.022
So that's straightforward.

00:40:42.022 --> 00:40:43.730
The service accounts
are slightly tricky.

00:40:43.730 --> 00:40:45.920
So they are
typically used if you

00:40:45.920 --> 00:40:51.200
want to perform an operation
on behalf of another user,

00:40:51.200 --> 00:40:52.520
on behalf of an application.

00:40:52.520 --> 00:40:55.870
That's when service
accounts are typically used.

00:40:55.870 --> 00:40:58.600
You can use it as an identity
as well as a resource.

00:40:58.600 --> 00:41:00.310
So what I mean by
that is so let's

00:41:00.310 --> 00:41:02.620
say I want to have some
kind of a notification

00:41:02.620 --> 00:41:04.507
or an automated job
that I want to be

00:41:04.507 --> 00:41:05.590
running in the background.

00:41:05.590 --> 00:41:07.980
I don't want to be running
it as an individual user.

00:41:07.980 --> 00:41:09.730
That's when the concept
of service account

00:41:09.730 --> 00:41:11.320
actually applies.

00:41:11.320 --> 00:41:14.630
So it's very critical
at a high level,

00:41:14.630 --> 00:41:17.530
before you start moving all
your workloads to the cloud

00:41:17.530 --> 00:41:19.900
or before you set up your
workloads on the cloud,

00:41:19.900 --> 00:41:22.510
to ensure that you have the
IAM structure built out,

00:41:22.510 --> 00:41:25.420
you have your users, roles,
and policies that are clearly

00:41:25.420 --> 00:41:28.780
mapped out, and then you
start migrating your data

00:41:28.780 --> 00:41:33.100
and set up your
projects in place.

00:41:33.100 --> 00:41:35.950
So, Rob, anything
specific from an identity

00:41:35.950 --> 00:41:38.140
perspective that
Google is working on,

00:41:38.140 --> 00:41:39.580
any of the enhancements?

00:41:39.580 --> 00:41:42.030
ROB SADOWSKI: Well, I think
you gave a good overview here.

00:41:42.030 --> 00:41:44.250
I think one thing that
I talked about in terms

00:41:44.250 --> 00:41:46.170
of policy intelligence,
I talked a little bit

00:41:46.170 --> 00:41:48.720
about making recommendations.

00:41:48.720 --> 00:41:51.330
There is also a
complementary piece

00:41:51.330 --> 00:41:54.300
called Access Troubleshooter
that, as you set up roles

00:41:54.300 --> 00:41:56.772
and as you set up permissions,
if something isn't

00:41:56.772 --> 00:41:58.230
able to be accessed,
Troubleshooter

00:41:58.230 --> 00:42:01.050
can help you walk through
and figure out exactly why.

00:42:01.050 --> 00:42:04.890
So you don't wind up saying just
grant all privileges, right?

00:42:04.890 --> 00:42:09.245
So you can continue to adhere
to those particular things.

00:42:09.245 --> 00:42:11.370
And then we've made a number
of other announcements

00:42:11.370 --> 00:42:14.070
around our own cloud
identity, where a lot of times

00:42:14.070 --> 00:42:17.850
we set up those accounts, or the
ability to use Active Directory

00:42:17.850 --> 00:42:19.275
and use that as a
managed service.

00:42:19.275 --> 00:42:21.280
So those were a
couple of things,

00:42:21.280 --> 00:42:23.460
but I think especially
in terms of what

00:42:23.460 --> 00:42:25.110
we doing around
policy intelligence,

00:42:25.110 --> 00:42:27.030
helping you get
those permissions

00:42:27.030 --> 00:42:30.480
and helping you make sure that
you are continuing to enforce

00:42:30.480 --> 00:42:32.970
things like lease
privilege effectively

00:42:32.970 --> 00:42:35.784
across multiple things.

00:42:35.784 --> 00:42:37.700
GOKUL RAGHURAMAN: That's
actually very good,

00:42:37.700 --> 00:42:40.930
because what we've noticed is a
lot of the times our users have

00:42:40.930 --> 00:42:44.320
set up there predefined roles
and the users are already

00:42:44.320 --> 00:42:44.970
set up.

00:42:44.970 --> 00:42:47.390
But a lot of times, they're
not even using those roles.

00:42:47.390 --> 00:42:51.730
So the idea is to have
fine-grained roles and policies

00:42:51.730 --> 00:42:54.220
associated to that.

00:42:54.220 --> 00:42:57.515
A lot of our clients start with
the traditional primitive rules

00:42:57.515 --> 00:42:59.140
which are no longer
recommended, right?

00:42:59.140 --> 00:43:01.048
So then you have the
predefined roles,

00:43:01.048 --> 00:43:03.340
which gives you those bunching
of those policies, which

00:43:03.340 --> 00:43:04.060
is good.

00:43:04.060 --> 00:43:05.890
But really the
custom rolls is where

00:43:05.890 --> 00:43:07.800
you get the full power
of Google, right?

00:43:07.800 --> 00:43:11.440
You'll be able to automate
exactly what permission

00:43:11.440 --> 00:43:14.230
set a particular user
is required to use.

00:43:14.230 --> 00:43:17.230
With this new implementation
that Google has come out

00:43:17.230 --> 00:43:19.750
with, you already have a way
that Google is going to tell

00:43:19.750 --> 00:43:21.640
you, hey, you have this user.

00:43:21.640 --> 00:43:23.320
You have assigned
these permissions,

00:43:23.320 --> 00:43:24.310
but it's not required.

00:43:24.310 --> 00:43:26.860
You're not using them,
so start taking it back.

00:43:26.860 --> 00:43:29.972
So that's very helpful.

00:43:29.972 --> 00:43:30.930
SHAHED LATIF: Go ahead.

00:43:30.930 --> 00:43:31.110
ROB SADOWSKI: Yeah.

00:43:31.110 --> 00:43:33.060
I mean, I think the
other analog Shahed

00:43:33.060 --> 00:43:37.470
is so now we understand and
are trying to gain control

00:43:37.470 --> 00:43:40.780
over those users, but we also
have to audit that activity.

00:43:40.780 --> 00:43:43.010
And so monitoring and
logging is something, Shahed,

00:43:43.010 --> 00:43:44.970
you know, you get referred
to at the beginning.

00:43:44.970 --> 00:43:47.490
What are your kind of best
practices or thinking around

00:43:47.490 --> 00:43:50.590
that as you work with clients
around monitoring, logging,

00:43:50.590 --> 00:43:53.612
and being able to provide
that documentation?

00:43:53.612 --> 00:43:54.820
GOKUL RAGHURAMAN: Absolutely.

00:43:54.820 --> 00:43:58.470
So traditionally,
all of our customers

00:43:58.470 --> 00:44:01.010
have some kind of a logging and
monitoring solution on-prem,

00:44:01.010 --> 00:44:01.510
right?

00:44:01.510 --> 00:44:03.840
So they want to leverage
the same solution as they

00:44:03.840 --> 00:44:04.860
go to the cloud as well.

00:44:04.860 --> 00:44:06.660
And a lot of times,
that will work.

00:44:06.660 --> 00:44:09.780
But by default, Google
already provides you

00:44:09.780 --> 00:44:11.500
capabilities like
Stackdriver, which

00:44:11.500 --> 00:44:14.450
will let you look at the logs.

00:44:14.450 --> 00:44:16.290
The logs are
automatically enabled.

00:44:16.290 --> 00:44:18.360
They're automatically
stored in Stackdriver.

00:44:18.360 --> 00:44:21.600
And you have a way to collect
it, export it, and then do

00:44:21.600 --> 00:44:23.040
some analytics on top of it.

00:44:23.040 --> 00:44:25.260
So at the base
infrastructure layer level,

00:44:25.260 --> 00:44:27.060
you already have tools
like Stackdriver,

00:44:27.060 --> 00:44:28.235
which will let you do that.

00:44:28.235 --> 00:44:29.610
Then you'll need
to start looking

00:44:29.610 --> 00:44:31.243
at what kind of
application level logs

00:44:31.243 --> 00:44:32.910
and monitoring that
I'll have to manage.

00:44:32.910 --> 00:44:35.460
And then finally, you look at
it from a business transparency

00:44:35.460 --> 00:44:36.230
perspective.

00:44:36.230 --> 00:44:37.980
What are the business
metrics and the logs

00:44:37.980 --> 00:44:39.953
that I need to be
starting to collect?

00:44:39.953 --> 00:44:42.120
So those are the three
levels that I would typically

00:44:42.120 --> 00:44:43.360
start looking at.

00:44:43.360 --> 00:44:45.608
The other good thing
in Google, and I

00:44:45.608 --> 00:44:48.150
know you can talk a little bit
about the access transparency,

00:44:48.150 --> 00:44:51.510
but really when we
go to a customer--

00:44:51.510 --> 00:44:53.700
and it goes back to how
we structure our projects.

00:44:53.700 --> 00:44:56.940
So typically, we would have
security SecOps engineering

00:44:56.940 --> 00:45:00.383
team, which is focused on
primarily looking at the audit

00:45:00.383 --> 00:45:02.800
events, looking at the security
events that are happening.

00:45:02.800 --> 00:45:05.370
So the way that you
structure your projects,

00:45:05.370 --> 00:45:06.700
again, is very critical.

00:45:06.700 --> 00:45:08.370
So you would have
your engineering teams

00:45:08.370 --> 00:45:10.530
focus on a separate
project structure.

00:45:10.530 --> 00:45:13.050
All your security logging
and all those logs

00:45:13.050 --> 00:45:15.145
will be managed on
a separate project.

00:45:15.145 --> 00:45:16.770
And only your security
engineering team

00:45:16.770 --> 00:45:17.850
gets access to that.

00:45:17.850 --> 00:45:20.260
So that is clear
separation of duties.

00:45:20.260 --> 00:45:23.760
You have a way to monitor
who gets access to the logs.

00:45:23.760 --> 00:45:27.450
And you can it report it on if
there is unauthorized access

00:45:27.450 --> 00:45:29.120
to the logs.

00:45:29.120 --> 00:45:32.150
Again, some of the things that
we have seen as best practice

00:45:32.150 --> 00:45:34.400
is go with fine-grained roles.

00:45:34.400 --> 00:45:36.260
Give absolutely
minimum permissions.

00:45:36.260 --> 00:45:38.870
Start with the editor
role, Because editor

00:45:38.870 --> 00:45:40.460
is required in a
lot of cases for you

00:45:40.460 --> 00:45:43.218
to be able to manage
the logs and do

00:45:43.218 --> 00:45:45.010
some kind of instrumentation
from the logs.

00:45:45.010 --> 00:45:50.540
So start there, and then
expand from those capabilities.

00:45:50.540 --> 00:45:52.700
By default, also, another
question that comes up

00:45:52.700 --> 00:45:56.450
is our customers ask, I
stored all these logs.

00:45:56.450 --> 00:45:58.070
Are these logs encrypted?

00:45:58.070 --> 00:45:59.810
Are they stored
in a safe manner?

00:45:59.810 --> 00:46:03.410
By default, when they are stored
in your cloud storage buckets,

00:46:03.410 --> 00:46:05.030
they are all
encrypted by default.

00:46:05.030 --> 00:46:08.180
You have a way to actually
use your secret management

00:46:08.180 --> 00:46:11.870
capabilities to manage
access to those logs.

00:46:11.870 --> 00:46:14.960
So those are some of the
high level best practices

00:46:14.960 --> 00:46:17.660
that we recommend as we look
at logging and monitoring

00:46:17.660 --> 00:46:20.090
as we work with our clients.

00:46:20.090 --> 00:46:22.890
Do you want to talk a little bit
about what Google has provided?

00:46:22.890 --> 00:46:23.090
ROB SADOWSKI: Yeah.

00:46:23.090 --> 00:46:24.620
I mean, I think
that just one thing

00:46:24.620 --> 00:46:26.943
you talked about, separation
of logs by project

00:46:26.943 --> 00:46:28.610
and being able to get
that granularity--

00:46:28.610 --> 00:46:30.770
I think everyone,
if you're not aware,

00:46:30.770 --> 00:46:33.530
there are actually when we think
about Cloud Audit Logging three

00:46:33.530 --> 00:46:34.490
distinct set of logs.

00:46:34.490 --> 00:46:35.990
There are admin
activity logs, which

00:46:35.990 --> 00:46:37.948
allow you to see what
administrators are doing.

00:46:37.948 --> 00:46:40.970
There's system event logs which,
if you're starting something

00:46:40.970 --> 00:46:43.012
on Compute Engine, what
are the different things?

00:46:43.012 --> 00:46:44.390
Like if a live
migration happens,

00:46:44.390 --> 00:46:46.160
when did that
happen, for example?

00:46:46.160 --> 00:46:47.790
There are also data access logs.

00:46:47.790 --> 00:46:50.868
So you can see not only from
an administrative perspective,

00:46:50.868 --> 00:46:52.535
but also from a user
perspective, people

00:46:52.535 --> 00:46:54.140
who have roles or
permissions, when

00:46:54.140 --> 00:46:55.680
that data has been accessed.

00:46:55.680 --> 00:46:58.760
So there's a nice real
separation between those.

00:46:58.760 --> 00:47:00.680
So depending on what
you're looking for

00:47:00.680 --> 00:47:03.375
and what you are going to have
to provide documentation on,

00:47:03.375 --> 00:47:05.250
the different types of
logs can support that.

00:47:05.250 --> 00:47:07.680
And then we also augment.

00:47:07.680 --> 00:47:09.140
You know, that's
for your own users

00:47:09.140 --> 00:47:10.307
and your own administrators.

00:47:10.307 --> 00:47:13.110
But we augment that with
access transparency logs.

00:47:13.110 --> 00:47:16.080
So this is an optional
feature that you can turn on,

00:47:16.080 --> 00:47:19.130
which allows you to see if
Google support engineers

00:47:19.130 --> 00:47:21.690
or administrators are
accessing any of your data.

00:47:21.690 --> 00:47:25.040
So that helps with the
overall understanding

00:47:25.040 --> 00:47:26.750
of what's happening
in my environment,

00:47:26.750 --> 00:47:30.870
being able to provide a full
log or near full log of access

00:47:30.870 --> 00:47:31.820
of that.

00:47:31.820 --> 00:47:33.842
And so that kind of
complements that piece.

00:47:33.842 --> 00:47:35.300
And another thing
we talked about--

00:47:35.300 --> 00:47:39.320
that many organizations
have existing log management

00:47:39.320 --> 00:47:42.710
systems and capabilities,
things like a Splunk

00:47:42.710 --> 00:47:45.760
or other systems like that in
Cloud Security Command Center

00:47:45.760 --> 00:47:51.020
with actually added
capabilities in the GA release

00:47:51.020 --> 00:47:54.240
to support direct connection
to that with this Splunk

00:47:54.240 --> 00:47:54.740
connector.

00:47:54.740 --> 00:47:56.360
So if that's where
you're doing some

00:47:56.360 --> 00:47:59.760
of the reporting and auditing,
you can get events from there.

00:47:59.760 --> 00:48:03.200
So yeah-- I mean, clearly
an area that we have focus.

00:48:03.200 --> 00:48:05.870
So maybe to wrap
this up, Shahed,

00:48:05.870 --> 00:48:08.990
are there specific tools or
frameworks or other things

00:48:08.990 --> 00:48:12.830
that you use specifically
with your clients

00:48:12.830 --> 00:48:14.528
in driving through
some of these issues?

00:48:14.528 --> 00:48:15.320
SHAHED LATIF: Sure.

00:48:15.320 --> 00:48:17.445
I've just summarized them
here quickly on the slide

00:48:17.445 --> 00:48:18.780
to speed up our discussion.

00:48:18.780 --> 00:48:21.957
So as I mentioned before,
and we gave a very high level

00:48:21.957 --> 00:48:23.540
representation on
the earliest slides,

00:48:23.540 --> 00:48:25.903
but we created an
architectural blueprint.

00:48:25.903 --> 00:48:27.320
We've actually
worked with clients

00:48:27.320 --> 00:48:28.520
where they've asked
us, tell us what

00:48:28.520 --> 00:48:30.603
good looks like from an
architectural perspective.

00:48:30.603 --> 00:48:33.290
So we already have pre-designed
templates around what

00:48:33.290 --> 00:48:34.760
architecture should look like.

00:48:34.760 --> 00:48:36.990
We then tailor that to
the needs of the client.

00:48:36.990 --> 00:48:38.840
And that's been a
huge success for us

00:48:38.840 --> 00:48:40.560
in trying to
accelerate the program.

00:48:40.560 --> 00:48:43.370
And that, again-- process,
controls, and technology,

00:48:43.370 --> 00:48:45.512
we look at all three elements.

00:48:45.512 --> 00:48:46.970
We also make sure
that the controls

00:48:46.970 --> 00:48:48.950
that we apply distinguish
between what's

00:48:48.950 --> 00:48:50.810
available in Google
and what's not.

00:48:50.810 --> 00:48:52.310
And that's really
important to know,

00:48:52.310 --> 00:48:55.490
because we've also developed
an understanding of the vendor

00:48:55.490 --> 00:48:56.468
ecosystem.

00:48:56.468 --> 00:48:58.760
A lot of our clients say,
what is the best tool to use?

00:48:58.760 --> 00:49:00.668
What's the right
thing to use here?

00:49:00.668 --> 00:49:02.210
We developed a point
of view on that.

00:49:02.210 --> 00:49:05.120
We've developed a
selection of criteria

00:49:05.120 --> 00:49:07.610
against different
functions and then

00:49:07.610 --> 00:49:10.398
a [INAUDIBLE] analysis around
that and developed a portfolio.

00:49:10.398 --> 00:49:11.940
It's hard to keep
up, because there's

00:49:11.940 --> 00:49:14.670
a lot of new third party
products out on the market.

00:49:14.670 --> 00:49:17.197
But we've got that as
well at our disposal.

00:49:17.197 --> 00:49:19.280
The other thing we have
is we've developed a tool.

00:49:19.280 --> 00:49:22.520
Because, often, we can put a
tool that's already available,

00:49:22.520 --> 00:49:24.405
but it takes time
to put that there.

00:49:24.405 --> 00:49:26.030
So we developed some
scripts of our own

00:49:26.030 --> 00:49:27.530
based, again, on
benchmarks that are

00:49:27.530 --> 00:49:29.540
available in the
industry, CIS namely.

00:49:29.540 --> 00:49:31.130
And we run our
scripts and quickly

00:49:31.130 --> 00:49:32.797
find out whether the
things are hardened

00:49:32.797 --> 00:49:34.530
the right way in the VM level.

00:49:34.530 --> 00:49:37.190
And then finally,
best practices--

00:49:37.190 --> 00:49:39.410
because we've been
to a few clients now,

00:49:39.410 --> 00:49:41.240
we've captured best practices.

00:49:41.240 --> 00:49:43.382
So we don't have to
repeat the best practices.

00:49:43.382 --> 00:49:45.090
It's hard to keep up
with all the changes

00:49:45.090 --> 00:49:46.340
that are going on with Google.

00:49:46.340 --> 00:49:49.093
But as we do, we keep
updating for that as well.

00:49:49.093 --> 00:49:50.760
ROB SADOWSKI: So we're
almost done here.

00:49:50.760 --> 00:49:53.210
You know, Gokul
or Shahed, if you

00:49:53.210 --> 00:49:57.370
had to make a couple of key
takeaways or recommendations

00:49:57.370 --> 00:50:00.700
around this and architecting,
you know, what would they be?

00:50:00.700 --> 00:50:04.020
SHAHED LATIF: I think it's
these four, five basic things.

00:50:04.020 --> 00:50:05.782
Understand your
regulatory environment.

00:50:05.782 --> 00:50:06.740
And understand it well.

00:50:06.740 --> 00:50:08.365
And what does that
really mean for you?

00:50:08.365 --> 00:50:09.460
Assess the key risks.

00:50:09.460 --> 00:50:11.023
I didn't touch about
threat modeling.

00:50:11.023 --> 00:50:12.940
But threat modeling is
essential to understand

00:50:12.940 --> 00:50:15.340
what the new attack
services that the cloud can

00:50:15.340 --> 00:50:16.345
bring into you.

00:50:16.345 --> 00:50:17.720
Once you understand
those things,

00:50:17.720 --> 00:50:19.570
you've got the baselines
of understanding.

00:50:19.570 --> 00:50:21.610
Start to implement the
right architecture.

00:50:21.610 --> 00:50:24.670
Use pre-tested
architecture blueprints.

00:50:24.670 --> 00:50:26.148
Don't make it up.

00:50:26.148 --> 00:50:27.940
And then identify the
third party solutions

00:50:27.940 --> 00:50:29.705
to augment your security.

00:50:29.705 --> 00:50:31.330
Google Cloud does
provide a lot, but it

00:50:31.330 --> 00:50:32.780
doesn't provide everything.

00:50:32.780 --> 00:50:34.450
So make sure you
have that identified.

00:50:34.450 --> 00:50:37.340
And then have monitor and
audit directly addressed.

00:50:37.340 --> 00:50:38.840
If you address
these, you are well

00:50:38.840 --> 00:50:40.358
on the way to a secure cloud.

00:50:40.358 --> 00:50:41.650
ROB SADOWSKI: All right, great.

00:50:41.650 --> 00:50:44.990
Well, thank you all for coming
and listening to our dialogue.

00:50:44.990 --> 00:50:46.180
And thank our panelists.

00:50:46.180 --> 00:50:49.270
And have a good rest of
Next, not too much left.

00:50:49.270 --> 00:50:50.770
[APPLAUSE]

00:50:52.270 --> 00:50:55.020
